        emit RewardsClaimed(user, _rewardTokens[i], rewardAmount, false);
      }

      accruedRewards[user][_rewardTokens[i]] = 0;
    }
  }
```
Each iteration in the loop of this function can be divided into 2 steps: 
1. Transfer the `accruedRewards[user][_rewardTokens[i]]` amount of reward token to user
2. Set value of `accruedRewards[user][_rewardTokens[i]]` to `0`

This process is vulnerable since the value of storage variable is updated before the transferring. If the transferring has a callback function, it will create a opportuinity for attacker to steal the rewards.
If the `rewardToken` is just a standard ERC20, no issue detected. But incase `rewardToken` is a [ERC777](https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-777) which is a extension of ERC20 with callback function when transferring, attacker can abuse this kind of technique to steal the reward. 

