# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The fee calculation in `Vault.withdraw()` is wrong. It causes the user to pay more fees on every withdrawal than they normally should.

## Proof of Concept
```sol
    function withdraw(
        uint256 assets,
        address receiver,
        address owner
    ) public nonReentrant syncFeeCheckpoint returns (uint256 shares) {
        if (receiver == address(0)) revert InvalidReceiver();

        shares = convertToShares(assets);

        uint256 withdrawalFee = uint256(fees.withdrawal);

        uint256 feeShares = shares.mulDiv(
            withdrawalFee,
            1e18 - withdrawalFee,
            Math.Rounding.Down
        );

        shares += feeShares;

        if (msg.sender != owner)
            _approve(owner, msg.sender, allowance(owner, msg.sender) - shares);

        _burn(owner, shares);

        if (feeShares > 0) _mint(feeRecipient, feeShares);

        adapter.withdraw(assets, receiver, address(this));

        emit Withdraw(msg.sender, receiver, owner, assets, shares);
    }
```

Given that `1 asset` is equal to `1 share` (to make the math easier):
$assets = 100e18$
$shares = 100e18$
$withdrawalFee = 1e16$ (1%)
$feeShares = 100e18 * 1e16 / (1e18 - 1e16) =  1.010101e+18$
$sharesToBurn = 100e18 + 1.010101e+18 = 101.010101e18$


So for `100e18` assets the user wants to withdraw they have to burn`101.010101e18` shares instead of `101e18`.
## Tools Used
none

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Change to:
```sol
    uint256 feeShares = shares.mulDiv(
        withdrawalFee,
        1e18,
        Math.Rounding.Down
    );
```

